nccom-test invocation, old vs new -- inf2.xlarge (one device, two NeuronCores), 2026-09-11.

What this is: the command line kernels/collectives.py builds, run for real.
What this is NOT: NeuronLink. One device, so ranks 0 and 1 are its two cores;
every figure below is core-to-core on one chip. all_reduce and p2p_thrasher
still skip on this part (min_devices=2) -- the module was called directly
with a one-device list to exercise its invocation and parse.

Old: -r len(devices) ... -c <op>. `-c` is --check {random,all_ones}, so
argparse rejects the operation and nccom-test exits 2 before running.
Neither collective could ever have produced a row on any part.

New: -r <cores across devices> ... --non-interactive <op>, as the
2026-08-26 probe ran it. (The script's banner says "trn1 device": the
NeuronDevice object was built with arch trn1; the hardware is inf2.)

